course: everyday
lesson: 316
title: Caring for pets, snake bites, bears and the zoo
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A pet is not a toy you can put away when you are tired of it. It depends on its family every day of its life, so looking after it is a responsibility.
explain: Animals have feelings too. A dog or cat can feel jealous or scared when a new baby or a new pet arrives, so newcomers are introduced slowly, with an adult watching.
explain: Small animals in cages and hutches cannot walk away from the sun. A rabbit left in a sunny hutch on a hot day in Texas can overheat and die in a short time.
explain: Wild animals are different from pets. If you are bitten by a snake, call the emergency number and keep still. If you meet a bear in the woods of Canada or the USA, stay with the adults, do not run, talk calmly and back away slowly.
fact: When a new baby or a new pet joins the family, introduce the animal slowly and always with an adult watching, because animals can feel jealous or scared of newcomers.
fact: Looking after a pet is a responsibility for its whole life, even when you are tired of it.
fact: If a family really cannot keep a pet, they find it a new home through an animal shelter; abandoning a pet is cruel and against the law.
fact: When you play with a pet, let it catch the toy sometimes, because play that always ends in frustration makes animals stressed or snappy.
fact: Cages and hutches must be kept in the shade, because small animals can overheat and die quickly in the sun.
fact: After any snake bite, call the emergency number, stay calm and keep as still as you can.
fact: Never suck a snake bite or cut it: it does not get the venom out and it causes more harm.
fact: If you see a bear, stay with the adults, do not run, speak calmly and back away slowly.
fact: Running from a bear can make it chase you, and a bear runs much faster than a person.
fact: At the zoo, follow the signs, stay behind the barriers and never climb fences, because zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous; if you drop something into an enclosure, tell a zoo keeper.
fact: With small animals, always move slowly and speak softly, because animals feel safe with calm people.
exercise: A family brings home a new puppy, and they already have an old cat. How should the animals meet? | Slowly, a little at a time, with an adult watching | All at once, shut in one room together so they get used to each other fast | The cat should be put outside for a week | It does not matter how, they will sort it out
exercise: Why should a new baby be introduced to the family dog carefully? | The dog can feel jealous or scared of the newcomer | Dogs do not notice babies at all, so there is nothing to worry about | Babies can catch fleas just by looking at a dog | Dogs always love babies at first sight
exercise: A boy in Ohio is bored of feeding his hamster every day. What is the right thing to do? | Keep caring for it, because the pet depends on him | Let it go free in the park | Stop feeding it and see what happens | Leave the cage out in the garage, where he does not have to think about it
exercise: A family truly cannot look after their dog any longer. What should they do? | Find it a new home through an animal shelter | Leave it by the side of a country road | Let it loose so it can find a new family | Keep it locked in the yard with no visits
exercise: Why is abandoning a pet wrong? | It is cruel to the animal and against the law | It is fine if the pet is clever enough | Pets are happier living free in the wild, like their wild cousins | It is only wrong for dogs, not for cats
exercise: A girl waves a feather toy for her kitten but always pulls it away before the kitten can catch it. What should she change? | Let the kitten catch the toy sometimes | Wave it higher so the kitten tries harder | Play only once a week | Tease the kitten until it gives up
exercise: What can happen if a pet never wins at play? | It can become stressed or snappy | It becomes calmer and happier | It learns to play by itself better | Nothing, pets do not mind losing
exercise: It is a hot summer day in Arizona and the guinea pig's cage is in full sun. What should you do? | Move the cage into the shade at once | Leave it, guinea pigs love the sunshine | Cover the cage with a dark blanket in the sun | Wait until evening to check on it
exercise: Why is a sunny hutch dangerous on a hot day? | Small animals can overheat and die quickly | The sun makes the hutch wood rot | Sunlight makes rabbits fall asleep | The animal will only get a little sunburnt
exercise: A man in Florida is bitten on the ankle by a snake. What should be done first? | Call the emergency number and keep him calm and still | Cut the bite open and suck out the venom before it spreads | Run quickly to the nearest house | Catch the snake to see what kind it is
exercise: Why should you never suck or cut a snake bite? | It does not remove the venom and it causes more harm | It does work, but only grown-ups are allowed to do it safely | Only cutting is wrong, sucking is helpful | It is fine if the snake was a small one
exercise: On a camping trip in Colorado your family sees a black bear near the trail. What should you do? | Stay with the adults, speak calmly and back away slowly | Run away as fast as you can | Climb the nearest small tree alone | Walk quietly closer to the bear to take a really good photo
exercise: Why should you never run from a bear? | Running can make the bear chase you | Bears are frightened of running people | Bears cannot see things that move | Running is fine if you are fast
exercise: At the zoo, a boy wants to climb the fence to get closer to the tigers. What is right? | Stay behind the barrier and follow the signs | Climb only if no keeper is looking | Climb if the tigers look sleepy | Reach one arm over to wave at them
exercise: Why are there barriers and fences at the zoo? | Zoo animals are wild and can be dangerous | Zoo animals are tame and just shy | Fences are only there for decoration | They keep the animals from getting bored
exercise: You want to say hello to a friend's pet rabbit. How should you come near it? | Move slowly and speak softly | Run up to it to show you are friendly | Clap your hands so it notices you | Pick it up quickly before it hops away
exercise: Why do small animals like people who are gentle and calm? | Calm people make animals feel safe | Animals only like people who are loud | Gentle people smell like food | Small animals cannot hear soft voices
test: A family gets a second cat. Which plan is best? | Let the cats meet slowly over several days, with an adult watching | Put both cats in one box together for an hour, so they are forced to make friends | Let the new cat take the old cat's bed and bowl | Keep the old cat away from the family for a month
test: A girl says, «I don't want my goldfish any more, I'll tip it into the pond in the park.» What is TRUE? | That is abandoning a pet; it is cruel and against the law | That is kind, because the fish will be free and will make friends with the other fish | It is fine, because fish have no feelings at all | It is allowed if the pond is big enough
test: A boy plays tug with his dog and never once lets go of the rope. Later the dog starts to growl and snap. What is the most likely reason? | The dog is frustrated because play always ends in losing | The dog wants to play for even longer and is asking for more | The dog is ill and has caught a cold | The dog is happy and showing it
test: A family goes out for the day in summer and leaves the rabbit's hutch in the sun. What is the danger? | The rabbit can overheat and die | The rabbit will only feel a bit sleepy | The rabbit will get a healthy suntan | There is no danger, rabbits come from deserts
test: A friend in Queensland says that after a snake bite you should cut the bite and suck the poison out. What is TRUE? | That is wrong: call the emergency number and keep the person still | That is right, as long as you do it in the very first minute after the bite | Only sucking is right, cutting is too much | It is right for small snakes only
test: Hiking in Alaska, a family meets a brown bear on the path. Which action is safest? | Stay together, speak calmly and back away slowly | Scream and run in different directions | Throw food at the bear and walk past it | Lie down and pretend to sleep straight away
test: A girl at the zoo drops her hat into the lion's enclosure. What should she do? | Tell a zoo keeper and stay behind the barrier | Climb the fence quickly while the lions sleep | Use a long stick to reach over the fence | Ask a friend to hold her while she leans over
test: A small boy runs shouting towards a duck at the farm, and the duck flaps away. What would help next time? | Moving slowly and speaking softly | Running faster so the duck cannot escape | Shouting louder so the duck knows he is there | Holding out his hands to grab it
test: Which is TRUE about having a pet? | You look after it all its life, even when you are tired of it | You can stop caring for it once it is old, or when you are bored of it | A pet only needs care on school holidays | A pet looks after itself after the first year
explain: A hiker in Arizona was bitten by a rattlesnake; his friends kept him still and called 911, and he recovered in hospital with antivenom.
explain: A family in Canada got out of their car to take a photo near a bear; the bear charged, and they only just made it back to the car.
fact: Antivenom given in hospital treats snake bites; cutting or sucking the bite does not help and can harm.
fact: Bears can run faster than a person and are dangerous even when they look calm.
test: mate got bit by a snake on the trail | Keep him still, call the emergency number | Suck the poison out | Cut the bite open | Run him to the car fast
test: bear by the road!! can we get out for a pic | No, stay in the car | Yes, if it's eating | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, from a few metres
test: we're tired of the puppy, can we just leave it in the woods | No, that's cruel and illegal; find it a new home through a shelter | Yes, it'll find food | Yes, dogs like the woods | Yes, if it's sunny
test: did u hear a guy got bit by a snake hiking and his mate "cut it open and sucked the poison out like in films". lesson? | Never cut or suck a bite; call the emergency number and keep still | Sucking out venom saves lives, it's what they do in films for a reason | Cut the bite and wash it well | Run fast to the nearest town
test: my uncle says a kid climbed into the lion bit at the zoo and "the lion just licked him". what's the bit to doubt | The friendly lion; zoo animals are wild and dangerous | That zoos have lions | That barriers keep people out | That zoos have signs
test: kids say the family that left their dog by the road "should go to jail" and are saying who it was. spread it? | No, don't spread who it was; tell an adult who can report it | Yes, name them everywhere | Yes, and go to their house | Yes, it's justice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the treatment that assists a person bitten by a snake? | Antivenom given in hospital, while the person keeps calm and still | Cutting across the bite with a clean knife, in order to release the venom from the wound | Sucking the venom from the wound | Running to make the blood carry it away
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person shall not run upon seeing a bear. | Running may cause the bear to chase, and it runs faster than a person | Bears cannot see persons who stand still, but they notice those who move about | Running is impolite in a national park | Bears are attracted only by bright colours
exam: It is given that a family can no longer keep a pet. Select the response consistent with the law and with kindness. | A new home shall be found through an animal shelter | The pet shall be released into the countryside, where it may live freely | The pet shall be left at a park | The pet shall be kept outdoors without care
exam: State the conduct expected at a zoo. | Following the signs and remaining behind the barriers | Climbing fences for a closer view | Feeding the animals through the bars | Calling loudly to wake the animals
